Effect 4 combines failures by typed reason. Cleanup has its own internal error
tag before the program forms one public error; otherwise two same-tag failures
can lose the cleanup detail. Tests assert both the main failure and cleanup
evidence. Loopback server tests need an execution environment that permits
local port binding.

Host boundaries use the matching Effect platform service. Web callbacks may
adapt `Request` and `Response`, but the owned Effect, runtime and abort signal
must remain connected. Native `AbortController` is used only at the host edge
that creates a Web signal; cancellation inside the program uses Effect
interruption and the Effect HTTP client.

## Deployment graph tests

Graph tests cover stage ownership, retained shared datasets, dashboard
definitions, Redacted secrets and the metrics-token qualification Action.
Workflow tests cover the exact commit passed from quality, trusted Preview
admission, native deploy and exact PR-stage cleanup.

Production uses the named GitHub Environment and the accepted main SHA.
There are no custom provider, public-route, dashboard or marker verification
steps. Local tests do not prove a hosted deployment.

## Route and application proof

Local route tests prove handler and response policy. The generated critical
journey inventory is owned by
[`packages/route-verification/src/critical-journey.ts`](../../packages/route-verification/src/critical-journey.ts)
and generated to
[`../verification/critical-journeys.json`](../verification/critical-journeys.json).
Regenerate it after journey changes:

```bash
bun --filter @packages/scripts critical-journeys:write
```

Local route tests check HTML, machine routes, Markdown and Open Graph
siblings, API/RPC/MCP behaviour, cache headers and every discovered static
asset. A local build verifies generated files; only a deployed route check can
prove provider/public reachability.

Static page content changes also need compiler tests for invalid frontmatter,
unsupported Markdown and unsafe links, plus agreement checks against the real
`content/pages/*.md` sources. Check cold compilation and source refresh, then
the visible pages, metadata and Markdown siblings. A browser build must keep
the file-reading and Markdown parser dependencies out of client output.
These checks complement the existing route and SEO/AEO tests; post pipeline
tests retain their separate ownership.

The SEO/AEO catalogue audit compares independently decoded authored post
frontmatter with the post service, shared catalogue and each discovery view.
Rendered JSON-LD must decode through the full owning `JsonLdGraph` codec,

