# Testing and quality

This document owns the current quality model. Run the smallest check that owns
the change, then close with the repository gate. Always state what the result
proves and what it does not.

## Standard checks

| Change                             | Focused checks                                                                         |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| TypeScript or package contract     | package `check-types`, focused package tests                                           |
| Effect service, Layer or lifecycle | focused `@effect/vitest` tests, package types, root lint                               |
| UI or route                        | web tests, browser tests where visible behaviour changes, route verification           |
| Alchemy or workflow                | graph/codec/service tests, workflow contract tests, root infrastructure types and lint |
| Dashboard or telemetry             | dashboard definition tests, observability tests                                        |
| Documentation                      | `bun run docs:check`, `bun run test:skills`                                            |
| Repository closeout                | `bun run verification`, then broader test/build when the claim needs them              |

Common commands:

```bash
bun run check
bun run check-types
bun run docs:check
bun run test:skills
bun run test
bun run build
bun run verification
```

`bun run check` is strict: formatting, lint errors and advice warnings must all
be zero. Rules are fixed at the owning source, Schema, service or call shape.
Do not use suppressions, unsafe casts or type laundering to make a check green.

`bun run check-types` first checks root deployment files through
`tsconfig.infrastructure.json`, then checks every workspace package through
Turborepo. This prevents Alchemy entrypoints from
falling between package projects.

The root `turbo.json` owns shared task behavior. Package-local `turbo.json`
files own exceptions: Web hashes its build environment, Posts caches generated
`.source/**`, and Infrastructure caches only `dist/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo` from
its no-emit incremental type check. That compiler-state file is not a runtime
`dist` contract. The source-run Scripts package has no build task.

## Closeout gate

`bun run verification` checks the current checkout: lint, documentation, skill
policy, Changesets status, Knip and types. It does not read a retained source
manifest or proof packet. The hosted quality workflow checks out and builds the
same exact commit, and passes that SHA to the downstream delivery workflow.
Only the real build output crossing to the browser job is retained as a
SHA-qualified GitHub artifact.

## Test ownership

Effect-heavy tests use `@effect/vitest`. Fallible, asynchronous or stateful
dependencies are replaced with test Layers, not module mocks. Test Scope owns
servers, handlers, browser pages, temporary directories, processes and
`ManagedRuntime` instances.

Resource tests must prove:

- success cleanup;
- typed acquisition or use failure;
- interruption while work is pending;
- cleanup failure;
- combined use and cleanup failure when both can happen.

Effect 4 combines failures by typed reason. Cleanup has its own internal error
tag before the program forms one public error; otherwise two same-tag failures
can lose the cleanup detail. Tests assert both the main failure and cleanup
evidence. Loopback server tests need an execution environment that permits
local port binding.

Host boundaries use the matching Effect platform service. Web callbacks may
adapt `Request` and `Response`, but the owned Effect, runtime and abort signal
must remain connected. Native `AbortController` is used only at the host edge
that creates a Web signal; cancellation inside the program uses Effect
interruption and the Effect HTTP client.

## Deployment graph tests

Graph tests cover stage ownership, retained shared datasets, dashboard
definitions, Redacted secrets and the metrics-token qualification Action.
Workflow tests cover the exact commit passed from quality, trusted Preview
admission, native deploy and exact PR-stage cleanup.

Production uses the named GitHub Environment and the accepted main SHA.
There are no custom provider, public-route, dashboard or marker verification
steps. Local tests do not prove a hosted deployment.

## Route and application proof

Local route tests prove handler and response policy. The generated critical
journey inventory is owned by
[`packages/route-verification/src/critical-journey.ts`](../../packages/route-verification/src/critical-journey.ts)
and generated to
[`../verification/critical-journeys.json`](../verification/critical-journeys.json).
Regenerate it after journey changes:

```bash
bun --filter @packages/scripts critical-journeys:write
```

Local route tests check HTML, machine routes, Markdown and Open Graph
siblings, API/RPC/MCP behaviour, cache headers and every discovered static
asset. A local build verifies generated files; only a deployed route check can
prove provider/public reachability.

Static page content changes also need compiler tests for invalid frontmatter,
unsupported Markdown and unsafe links, plus agreement checks against the real
`content/pages/*.md` sources. Check cold compilation and source refresh, then
the visible pages, metadata and Markdown siblings. A browser build must keep
the file-reading and Markdown parser dependencies out of client output.
These checks complement the existing route and SEO/AEO tests; post pipeline
tests retain their separate ownership.

The SEO/AEO catalogue audit compares independently decoded authored post
frontmatter with the post service, shared catalogue and each discovery view.
Rendered JSON-LD must decode through the full owning `JsonLdGraph` codec,

